 "claim": {
  "headline": "On our synthetic benchmark (2 worlds, time-based split), at the same false-alarm rate (0.5% of legitimate transactions, thresholds fixed on validation), the ring-aware detector (G2+R) found 97% of mule rings, versus 97% for the one-payment-at-a-time model (B0) and 97% for the account-history baseline with ring grouping (B1+R); it found a similar share of rings as the one-payment-at-a-time model (paired 95% interval of the difference -9 points to +9 points, includes zero). Realised test false-alarm rates differed from the target by more than 10% for B0, B1+R, G2+R in at least one world (see the operating-point table).",
  "vs_strong_baseline": "Against the strong account-history baseline with the same ring layer (B1+R), G2+R found a similar share of rings as it (97% versus 97%; paired 95% interval of the difference +0 points to +0 points, includes zero; 0 rings caught only by G2+R and 0 only by B1+R).",
  "earliness": "Among the 34 rings caught by both, G2+R caught them at a similar time than B1+R (median paired difference +0.0 minutes; 95% interval +0.0 min to +0.0 min, includes zero).",
  "before_exit": "G2+R caught 94% of rings before their first cash-out (of 35 rings with a cash-out), versus 80% for B0.",
  "hops": "At the moment G2+R caught a ring, a median of 2 hop(s) remained before the first cash-out.",
  "weakest_typology": "The weakest typology for G2+R was slow roll (90% of 10 rings caught)."
 },
 "utc": "2026-10-17T20:08:47+00:00"
